EC2 stands for:

🖥️ Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud
E = Elastic
C = Compute
C = Cloud
Simple meaning

Amazon EC2 = A virtual computer/server in the AWS cloud.

Your Application
      ↓
Amazon EC2 🖥️
(Virtual Server)
      ↓
AWS Cloud ☁️

Elastic means you can increase or decrease computing resources based on your needs.

===

An AWS Instance usually means an Amazon EC2 instance.

Simple explanation 🖥️

An EC2 instance = a virtual computer/server running inside AWS cloud.

Think of it like this:

Your Laptop 💻
│
├── CPU
├── RAM
├── Storage
└── Operating System

AWS gives you a similar computer virtually:

AWS EC2 Instance ☁️
│
├── vCPU
├── RAM
├── Storage (EBS)
└── Operating System
Example

You can create an EC2 instance with:

Ubuntu/Linux
2 vCPU
2 GB RAM
Your application installed

Then deploy:

Your Python / Flask / FastAPI App
            ↓
       EC2 Instance
            ↓
        Internet 🌐
            ↓
          Users
Easy analogy

🏢 AWS Cloud = Building
🖥️ EC2 Instance = One rented computer inside that building

You start, stop, or terminate the instance when needed.

⚠️ Important: Some EC2 instance types and related resources can create charges, so always check the instance type and AWS billing/free-plan eligibility before launching one.

For learning:

S3 🪣 → Store files
EC2 🖥️ → Run a virtual server/application
SageMaker 🧠 → Build/train/deploy ML and AI models

